# Packet compression, server to client (O209; server/README.md "Packet compression (O209)"). The client engine already
# inflates: with the checksum on, a zero where the u32 checksum goes marks a body that is a raw deflate stream (one
# stream per connection, Z_SYNC_FLUSH per packet, the 00 00 FF FF tail cut off and added back by the client), inside the
# XTEA layer. This patch makes the server send such packets.
#   * order: body -> deflate -> u16 length -> XTEA (padded to 8) -> u32 0 instead of the adler word -> u16 length. The
#     XTEA layer, the length words and the checksum rule of plain packets are exactly as before.
#   * who: only a connection whose login carried the markers of the official client ("OTCv8TierByte" + "A"), only with
#     config.lua packetCompression = true (default false). Any other client, the login protocol, the status protocol and
#     the live cast keep plain packets. A client of an older release of ours inflates too (the code is the engine's).
#   * what: bodies of packetCompressionMin (default 128, at least 16) to 60000 bytes; level packetCompressionLevel
#     (default 3, 1-9). Smaller and larger bodies are sent plain (the client inflates only flagged packets). Once a
#     body is deflated it is sent deflated even if it did not shrink, because the client's stream must see every block.
#   * nothing is compressed from the client to the server and the server never inflates, so there is no decompression
#     bomb on this side. The client side limit is client base patch 0131.
#   * cost: z_stream per compressing connection, 2^14 window and memLevel 7: about 128 KB. CPU: server/tools/packet_compress_bench.py.
#   * a deflate error stops compression on that connection (later packets plain), nothing is dropped.
#   * build: links zlib (find_package(ZLIB) in CMakeLists.txt; a Visual Studio project needs zlib added by hand).
